{
  "result": "There should be a museum for things that almost happened. Not the grand alternatives of history, but the private branches: the train you nearly boarded, the apology you rehearsed, the yellow coat you left hanging in the shop because you thought you were not a yellow coat sort of person.\n\nThe first room would contain empty chairs. Beside each chair, a label would describe someone you might have met if rain had started five minutes later. Visitors would sit quietly, listening to recordings of conversations that never occurred. Some would laugh without knowing why. Others would suddenly feel less alone.\n\nUpstairs, behind glass, would be all the sentences abandoned halfway through writing. Love letters would curl like dried leaves. Resignations would stand stiffly at attention. Unsent invitations would glow a little after dark, attracting moths that the curators could never entirely explain or persuade to leave through the open windows.\n\nOf course, the museum would need a garden. There, gardeners would cultivate seeds from hobbies briefly considered: astronomy, pottery, the violin. Nothing would bloom according to season. A visitor might discover a whole orchard growing from an afternoon once spent wondering whether it was too late to learn something new.\n\nAt the exit, there would be no gift shop. Only a desk, a pencil, and a stack of blank cards. Take one, the attendant would say. There is still room in the collection. Then they would point toward the street, where ordinary life waited, unfinished, wearing its astonishing yellow coat.",
